silencio bruno that's just a dumb voice  in your head disney pixar's luca is a  film about friendship and perseverance  just like luca and alberto the creators  of this film faced numerous  obstacles from pandemic restrictions to  creating never seen before technology  the lengths that went into the creation  of this film were intense  luckily with the help of their epic cast  they were able to power through but not  without multiple silencio brunos of  course  disney and pixar have created some  animated staples like monsters inc nemo  and coco luca director enrico casarosa  followed directly in their footsteps by  inspiring the creation of new  groundbreaking technology while monsters  inc brought the ability to animate soft  hair and nemo water luca animators hit a  breakthrough when it came to the smooth  transition between human skin and fish  scales the question from day one  technically how do you go from fish  scales to human skin luca's  transformations throughout the film play  a major role and the entire team knew  that they needed to be perfect if they  wanted this film to be what enrico had  envisioned they used what is called a  transformation rig which allowed them to  turn their characters invisible now you  could lay the second character in and  make him invisible and leave just the  feet and you could pick how faded you  wanted that to be or where you wanted it  to be or not be and it was like whoa  this is so cool however cool the  technology may have been it didn't come  without its difficulties the  transformation of our sea monsters and  in particular luca was one of the  biggest challenges on our film they  initiated multiple tests and even had a  painter create a gif to try and bring  the image of the transformation they had  in their head to life after the ideas  and technology were floated around not  just the effects department but the  production team and artists they finally  figured out their masterpiece with a  little help from the animation used for  coco the blending aspects of it we  actually used a little bit of technology  from coco the new technology was even  used in developing the motion of pasta  in the film how italian after getting  the scales and human skin down packed it  was time for the animators to take a  crack at the characters because of  movement and facial expressions the two  departments needed to work together in  order to make sure the expressions of  the characters matched the  transformation does it come as an  explosion of excitement or does it  happen slowly we needed to be able to  control that they took into  consideration how the character would  react to a splash of water versus a body  of water and even analyzed marine  iguanas for inspiration on their  movements this all especially came into  play when they animated what they call  the swim sequence swim was the first  time where we were really showing off  what it means to be a sea monster and a  human and to switch back and forth  between the two this is where luca makes  the decision to go to the human world  originally the scene was supposed to be  comedic but they discovered the  potential in it after uncovering the  technology they needed for the sea  monsters in this scene they even used  the horizon to show luca's inner turmoil  the horizon line is always against luka  when he's struggling and it's for him  when he's actually getting it right  despite being a sea monster enrico made  it clear that luca should always appear  not creepy whoa whoa i don't want to be  bitten by this guy in order to do this  they made sure their creations were  always lyrical and decorative and made  sure to include enrico's love for  childlike animation even the sea monster  to human transformation was meant to be  gentle isn't it great  no  it's bad and i'm not supposed to be up  here  luckily for the animators they had  impeccable concept art drawn for them  enrico translated many of his own  experiences into the film one of those  being his love for japanese animation  specifically by hayao miyazaki he sat  down with his crew and watched projects  like the castle of cagliostro and future  boy conan we watched it together we  analyzed what can limited animation  inspire us to do so that inspiration  turned into beautiful 2d concept art  that later was translated into 3d  on-screen however director of  photography david bianchi said they  wanted to make sure that 3d animations  still reflected enrico's initial vision  it still feels like a cg film but you  can sense the hand of the artists  touching it everyone puerto rosa  pretends to believe in sea monsters  while the location of puerto rosso is  based on five fishing towns on the  coastal cinque terre region of italy the  name was also a nod to japanese  animation disney released a copyright  document confirming that the name was a  play on porco rosso a film about an  italian pig pilot from the 90s aside  from japanese influence enrico also used  his love for looney tunes and took  inspiration from how the cartoon  demonstrated speed we'd have three you  know from my hand here to here to here  we would do all three of them to kind of  simulate quick speed even sean the sheep  had a part in luca in terms of how the  creators wanted to incorporate some  stop-motion vibes into the film as for  the creation of the sea monsters those  came from old renaissance maps from the  1500s and old italian sculptures they  found when they went to italy during  their pre-production research overall  enrico wanted these animations to be  unique and imperfect and even had the  computer make mistakes on purpose to  achieve his vision i love the sense that  all of our characters in some way feel  different or unusual luca's director put  his soul into the film literally the  story is loosely based on his experience  growing up in genoa with his best friend  alberto my best friend alberto was a bit  of a troublemaker alberto pushed me out  of my comfort zone and pushed me off  many cliffs metaphorically and not the  film's theme of friendship and being who  we are goes so much deeper than the  audience knows  i think he needs me  you know  i can't do it without you while the  technical aspects faced many challenges  when it came to animations the actors  also faced difficulties themselves luca  is a part of the epic collection of  films that were made during the covet 19  pandemic shout out to raya and the last  dragon the three main characters luca  alberto and julia may have been the best  of friends on screen but off-screen  their actors rarely saw one another  because of protocols some actors like  jack dylan grazer were forced to record  their voiceovers in their mom's closet  it's fine i'm not human  while he may have needed to have worked  around some sweaters and shoes his  makeshift studio seemed pretty cozy but  despite his unique studio the scenario  was still difficult for him it was  definitely a stretch for me a challenge  for me as an actor and as just a human  being not to mention he may have annoyed  his neighbors just a tad i bet my  neighbors were really freaked out about  the amount of screaming that was going  on from my house i don't know what they  were thinking as for jacob trombley who  voices luca he was a little luckier than  jack i actually started recording before  covet hit so i was able to go into the  san francisco studio and they gave me  the tour when the pandemic did arrive he  was prepared to get his closet turned  into a studio but instead was able to  safely record in a studio located in his  home city of vancouver  underdogs forever emma berman who played  julia didn't get a whole tour like jacob  but was lucky enough to record her part  in the studio as well look  that's saturn it's my favorite  they didn't get the full filming  experience but the cast still fell in  love with their characters and the story  of luca while jack and jacob just  couldn't get over how beautiful the  animation of the film turned out the  color of the sea and the sparkliness of  the sea emma fell in love and related  wholeheartedly to the character of julia  i had a really fun time playing her  because i relate to her in a lot of ways  we were both passionate about what we do  and we're also very excited and joyful  people despite their challenges the cast  and crew transported their audience off  their living room couch to what jacob  trombley described as the most perfect  vacation that's literally what porterosa  is director casarosa was able to share  his message of love and friendship with  the world and used his creative mind to  help inspire technology that will be  used and developed for many more  animations to come talk about a  groundbreaking film it's another one in  the books for disney pixar thanks for  watching
 

